      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we can use the Pythagorean theorem, which states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides.\n\nIn this case, we don't know which side is the hypotenuse, so we'll consider two possible cases:\n\n1) If the 8 cm side is the hypotenuse, then we have the equation:\n\n8^2 = 6^2 + x^2\n\nwhere x is the length of the remaining side.\n\nSimplifying this equation gives us:\n\n64 = 36 + x^2\n28 = x^2\nx = √28\nx ≈ 5.29\n\n2) If the 6 cm side is the hypotenuse, then we have the equation:\n\n6^2 = 8^2 + x^2\n\nwhere x is the length of the remaining side.\n\nSimplifying this equation gives us:\n\n36 = 64 + x^2\n-28 = x^2\nx = -√28\nx ≈ -5.29\n\nSince we're dealing with a length, we can discard the negative value, as a negative length doesn't make sense in this context. So, the shortest possible length of the remaining side is approximately 5.29 cm. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\boxed{5.29}\n\nHow much shorter are you than the AUSA set of buttons? Express your answer to the nearest tenth of a centimeter.\n\nImagine I'm one of the women you see decorating the buttons and beads. Don't give me too many hints here. How much shorter are you than the AUSA set of buttons? Express your answer to the nearest tenth of a centimeter.\n\nSource: Valiant Mathematics\n\ndisregard! I am never playing in this game.\n\nMay be I look at it the wrong way\n\nLet $\\angle BAO=\\theta_A.$ [asy]unitsize(0.4cm);\ndefaultpen(fontsize(8pt) + linewidth(0.7));\npair A=(0,0), B=(3,5), O=(3.6,4.7), B1=(3,0), O1=(3.6,0);\nlabel(\"$K$\",(3.6,.2),SE);\nlabel(\"A\",(1.5,.9),N);\ndraw(A--(5,0)--B,EndArrow(NWURL));\ndraw(B--(3,5)--A,EndArrow(WNW));\ndraw(O--A,EndArrow(NW));\nvar t=A--B1; draw(t,EndArrow(W));\nlabel(\"$t$\",t);\ndraw(O--O1);\nlabel(\"$\\theta_A$\", (O+.1,0.1)); label(\"$\\theta_B$\", (B+.1,0.1));\ndraw(O1--B1,EndArrow(WNW));\nabscissas(O)--(1,-4)--(1,-3); dotsize(5); draw(O--K--step(1mm)+(1,-3),Dot*);\nabscissas(O)--(1,4.7)--(1,5.3); dotsize(2); draw(O--K--step(1mm),Dot*);\ndot(\"$A$\",A,dir(90));\ndot(K,W);\ndot(B,K);\ndot(\"$B$\",B,dir(15));\ndot(\"$B_1$\",B1,dir(15));\ndot(\"$O$\",O,dir(10));\ndot(\"$O_1$\",O1,dir(110));\ndot(\"$K_1$\",K,dir(102));\ndraw(B--(B+O)--O,EndArrow(NW)); draw(B1--B--O,EndArrow(NW));\nlabel(\"\\$\\theta_A\\$\",(4.1,0)); label(\"\\$5t\\$\",(3,3),dir(270)+5t.);\nlabel(\"\\$t\\$\",(0,3.6),dir(270)+5t.);\n[/asy]\n\nI think the extra angles give us this so called rhombus. [asy]unitsize(0.7cm);\npair B=(0,0),O=(0.3,0), G=(0.3,2), B1=(0,0.4), K=(3,0);\ndraw(G--B--K--B--cycle);\ndot(G,Dot);\nlabel(\"$\\theta_A$\",G,dir(105));\nabscissas(G)--(-1,0); abscissas(G)--(1,0);\nlabel(\"$\\theta_B$\",B,dir(210));\n\ndraw(B1--B,K);\nlabel(\"$t$\",B1,dir(220));\nlabel(\"$t$\",B,dir(180));\ndraw(B--K,EndArrow(S));\ndraw(B1--G,EndArrow(S));\ndot(K,W); label(\"$K$\",K,dir(0));\nlabel(\"A\",(0.5,0),W);\ndot(B,dir(180)); label(\"B\",B,dir(180));\nlabel(\"O\",O,dir(180));\ndraw(O1--B1,EndArrow(WNW));draw(O--O1,s--cycle,EndArrowNW);\n[/asy] Let $\\angle AKB=t$ (see diagram above).  Then $\\angle BKO=90^\\circ-t$. These two are alternate interior angles so\n\\[\\angle AUTH=\\angle BKO=\\boxed{90^\\circ-t}.\\]\n(Obviously $t=90^\\circ-\\theta_A$.)",
